WEBVTT

00:00:00.328 --> 00:00:04.640
SQL injection occurs when application input is used to construct a database query

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:05.680
unsafely,

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:07.590
allowing the input to alter query meaning.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.950
For JavaScript developers,

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:13.195
the highest-value prevention pattern is to avoid building SQL by combining strings with

00:00:13.195 --> 00:00:14.400
untrusted values.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.600
The preferred control is parameterized queries or prepared statements,

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.100
where values are passed separately from the query structure.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.300
Query builders and ORMs can reduce risk, but they should not be treated as magic shields.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.960
Many libraries are safe for common patterns and unsafe when developers drop into raw query

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:29.690
fragments,

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:32.485
dynamic clauses, custom sorting, or handwritten filters.

00:00:32.485 --> 00:00:35.100
The assessment question is not whether an ORM is present.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:39.200
The question is whether untrusted values can influence query structure rather than being

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:40.245
bound as values.

00:00:40.245 --> 00:00:42.000
Dynamic features need careful design.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.365
Sorting, filtering, pagination, search fields,

00:00:44.365 --> 00:00:48.380
and report builders often require parts of a query to change based on user choices.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:51.500
These are places where allowlists are more appropriate than free-form input.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.400
For example, a client can be allowed to select from known field names or directions,

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.655
while the application maps those choices to controlled query components.

00:00:58.655 --> 00:01:02.200
Input validation helps, but it does not replace safe query construction.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.900
Validation can reject values that do not match business expectations,

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.500
reduce accidental errors, and improve type safety.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.485
Parameterization addresses the database interpretation problem.

00:01:11.485 --> 00:01:15.615
Least-privileged database accounts add another layer by limiting what the application can

00:01:15.615 --> 00:01:17.090
do if a query path fails.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:19.885
Strong SQL injection prevention is boring by design.

00:01:19.885 --> 00:01:24.000
Use parameterized APIs, constrain dynamic query structure to known choices,

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.395
avoid raw string-built queries, review escape hatches in ORMs,

00:01:27.395 --> 00:01:30.800
and test high-risk paths such as search, filters, sort controls,

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.500
and administrative reporting.
